var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"legacy/tmsv/#tmsv_ref","page":"TMSV","text":"The tmsv module models a Two-Mode Squeezed Vacuum (TMSV) state — the simplest Gaussian entangled state. Both modes are produced by a single parametric interaction and characterized by the mean photon number μ. This source is primarily used as a building block for the SPDC and ZALM modules and as a validation baseline since it does not produce dual-rail Bell states.","title":"TMSV"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/tmsv/#Type","page":"TMSV","text":"","title":"Type"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/tmsv/#Source-Metrics","page":"TMSV","text":"","title":"Source Metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/tmsv/#Internal-Matrices","page":"TMSV","text":"","title":"Internal Matrices"},{"category":"type","location":"legacy/tmsv/#Genqo.tmsv.TMSV","page":"TMSV","text":"mutable struct TMSV\n\nParameters for a Two-Mode Squeezed Vacuum (TMSV) entanglement source.\n\nThe TMSV is the simplest Gaussian entangled state. Both modes are produced by a single parametric interaction, and the entanglement is characterized by the mean photon number μ.\n\nFields\n\nmean_photon::Real: Mean photon number per mode (default 1e-2)\ndetection_efficiency::Real: Detector efficiency, ∈ [0, 1] (default 1.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.tmsv.TMSV"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/tmsv/#Genqo.tmsv.probability_success","page":"TMSV","text":"probability_success(μ::Real, ηᵈ::Real) -> Real\n\n\nCalculate the probability of photon-photon state generation with the given parameters.\n\nParameters\n\nμ : The mean photon number of the TMSV state\nηᵈ : Detection efficiency\n\nReturns\n\nProbability of successful photon-photon state generation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.tmsv.probability_success"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/tmsv/#Genqo.tmsv.covariance_matrix","page":"TMSV","text":"covariance_matrix(μ::Real) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\n\nConstruct the covariance matrix for a TMSV state.\n\nParameters\n\nμ : The mean photon number of the TMSV state\n\nReturns\n\nThe covariance matrix for the TMSV state, in the qpqp ordering\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.tmsv.covariance_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/tmsv/#Genqo.tmsv.loss_matrix_pgen","page":"TMSV","text":"loss_matrix_pgen(ηᵈ::Real) -> Matrix{ComplexF64}\n\n\nConstruct the loss contribution to the A-matrix for TMSV probability-of-success calculations.\n\nParameters\n\nηᵈ: Detection efficiency, ∈ [0, 1]\n\nReturns\n\n8×8 ComplexF64 loss matrix for use in A = k_function_matrix(cov) + loss_matrix_pgen(ηᵈ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.tmsv.loss_matrix_pgen"},{"category":"section","location":"api/wick/#api_wick","page":"Wick Contraction","text":"The computational core. Gaussian moments are evaluated by Wick contraction: a symbolic moment polynomial is compiled once into WTerms, a type-stable representation grouped by monomial degree, and then contracted against the inverse kernel by W.\n\nMost users never call these directly — project and the metrics built on it drive them, and HybridProjectionEngine caches the compiled polynomials. They are documented because they are the extension point for new moment calculations.","title":"Wick Contraction"},{"category":"function","location":"api/wick/#Genqo.extract_W_terms","page":"Wick Contraction","text":"extract_W_terms(\n    C::AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}\n) -> WTerms\n\n\nPrecompile a Nemo polynomial into a WTerms object suitable for the fast W(::WTerms, invA) path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.extract_W_terms"},{"category":"type","location":"api/wick/#Genqo.WTerms","page":"Wick Contraction","text":"struct WTerms{B<:Tuple}\n\nA precompiled moment polynomial as a heterogeneous tuple of WBuckets, one per degree present in the polynomial. The tuple type carries each bucket's N at compile time so iteration unrolls and each _W_bucket call specializes on its bucket's degree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.WTerms"},{"category":"function","location":"api/wick/#Genqo.W","page":"Wick Contraction","text":"W(t::WTerms, invA::Matrix{ComplexF64}) -> ComplexF64\n\n\nFast Wick evaluator for precompiled moment terms.\n\n\n\n\n\nW(\n    C::AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem},\n    invA::Matrix{ComplexF64}\n) -> ComplexF64\n\n\nEvaluate a (symbolic) moment polynomial by Wick contraction against invA.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.W"},{"category":"function","location":"api/wick/#Genqo.wick_out","page":"Wick Contraction","text":"wick_out(\n    moment::Vector{Int64},\n    invA::Matrix{ComplexF64}\n) -> ComplexF64\n\n\nEvaluate a single monomial term via Wick's theorem (sum over perfect pairings).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.wick_out"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/spdc/#spdc_ref","page":"SPDC","text":"The spdc module models a source composed of two Two-Mode Squeezed Vacuum (TMSV) states whose idler modes are swapped, realizing what is colloquially called an \"SPDC\" entanglement source. This is not to be confused with the fact that there are two Spontaneous Parametric Down-Conversion (SPDC) processes occuring. This is a standard unheralded source of dual-rail entangled photon pairs.","title":"SPDC"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/spdc/#Type","page":"SPDC","text":"","title":"Type"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/spdc/#Source-Metrics","page":"SPDC","text":"","title":"Source Metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/spdc/#Internal-Matrices","page":"SPDC","text":"","title":"Internal Matrices"},{"category":"type","location":"legacy/spdc/#Genqo.spdc.SPDC","page":"SPDC","text":"mutable struct SPDC\n\nParameters for a Spontaneous Parametric Down-Conversion (SPDC) entanglement source.\n\nAn SPDC source is composed of two Two-Mode Squeezed Vacuum (TMSV) states whose idler modes are swapped. This is not to be confused with the fact that there are two Spontaneous Parametric Down-Conversion (SPDC) processes occuring. This is a standard unheralded source of dual-rail entangled photon pairs.\n\nFields\n\nmean_photon::Real: Mean photon number per mode (default 1e-2)\ndetection_efficiency::Real: Detector efficiency, ∈ [0, 1] (default 1.0)\nbsm_efficiency::Real: Bell-state measurement efficiency, ∈ [0, 1] (default 1.0)\noutcoupling_efficiency::Real: Photon outcoupling / transmission efficiency, ∈ [0, 1] (default 1.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.spdc.SPDC"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/spdc/#Genqo.spdc.fidelity","page":"SPDC","text":"fidelity(μ::Real, ηᵗ::Real, ηᵈ::Real) -> Real\n\n\nCalculate the Bell-state fidelity of the single-mode SPDC source under loss.\n\nThis computes the overlap ⟨Φ|ρ|Φ⟩ of the photon-photon state produced by the SPDC source with an ideal Bell state.\n\nParameters\n\nμ  : Mean photon number\nηᵗ : Outcoupling / transmission efficiency\nηᵈ : Detection efficiency\n\nReturns\n\nReal-valued Bell-state fidelity of the SPDC source for the given parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.spdc.fidelity"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/spdc/#Genqo.spdc.spin_density_matrix","page":"SPDC","text":"spin_density_matrix(\n    μ::Real,\n    ηᵗ::Real,\n    ηᵈ::Real,\n    nvec::Vector{Int64}\n) -> Matrix{ComplexF64}\n\n\nCalculate the 4×4 spin-spin density matrix for the SPDC source conditioned on photon-number measurement outcome nvec after simulated mode-memory interaction.\n\nParameters\n\nμ    : Mean photon number per mode\nηᵗ   : Outcoupling / transmission efficiency\nηᵈ   : Detection efficiency\nnvec : Photon-number vector [n₁, n₂, n₃, n₄] for the four modes\n\nReturns\n\n4×4 ComplexF64 normalized spin-spin density matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.spdc.spin_density_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/spdc/#Genqo.spdc.covariance_matrix","page":"SPDC","text":"covariance_matrix(μ::Real) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\n\nConstruct the 8×8 covariance matrix for an SPDC source.\n\nParameters\n\nμ: Mean photon number per mode\n\nReturns\n\n8×8 Float64 covariance matrix in qpqp ordering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.spdc.covariance_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/spdc/#Genqo.spdc.loss_bsm_matrix_fid","page":"SPDC","text":"loss_bsm_matrix_fid(\n    ηᵗ::Real,\n    ηᵈ::Real\n) -> Matrix{ComplexF64}\n\n\nConstruct the 16×16 loss matrix for SPDC fidelity calculations.\n\nEncodes the combined transmission-detection loss η = ηᵗηᵈ for all four signal modes. The resulting matrix is added to the K-matrix before Wick evaluation of Bell-state overlap terms.\n\nParameters\n\nηᵗ: Outcoupling / transmission efficiency, ∈ [0, 1]\nηᵈ: Detection efficiency, ∈ [0, 1]\n\nReturns\n\n16×16 ComplexF64 loss matrix for fidelity: A = k_function_matrix(cov) + loss_bsm_matrix_fid(ηᵗ, ηᵈ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.spdc.loss_bsm_matrix_fid"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/spdc/#Genqo.spdc.dmijZ","page":"SPDC","text":"dmijZ(\n    dmi::Int64,\n    dmj::Int64,\n    Ainv::Matrix{ComplexF64},\n    nvec::Vector{Int64},\n    ηᵗ::Real,\n    ηᵈ::Real\n) -> ComplexF64\n\n\nCalculate a single element of the unnormalized spin-spin density matrix for the SPDC source.\n\nParameters\n\ndmi  : Row index of the density matrix element (1–4, indexing the four Bell states)\ndmj  : Column index of the density matrix element (1–4)\nAinv : Inverse A-matrix (from k_function_matrix + loss_bsm_matrix_fid)\nnvec : Photon-number vector [n₁, n₂, n₃, n₄] for the four modes\nηᵗ   : Outcoupling / transmission efficiency\nηᵈ   : Detection efficiency\n\nReturns\n\nComplex density matrix element ρ[dmi, dmj] (unnormalized).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.spdc.dmijZ"},{"category":"section","location":"api/clicks/#api_clicks","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"Two distinct things are described in the photon-number basis, and they are not interchangeable.\n\nA projector is what the detectors did. It is an outcome, built with projector, and it may leave modes undetected (:). It is idempotent, so it can only be added to other projectors, never scaled.\n\nA click state is a target to compare against. It is built with clicks, lives only on the free modes, and carries amplitudes, so it has a full bra-ket algebra. Click states are what you pass to fidelity and dot.\n\nΠ = projector([1, 0, :])            # detected 1 photon, 0 photons, one mode left free\nψ = (clicks([1]) + clicks([0]))/√2  # a target over that free mode","title":"Click States and Projectors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/clicks/#Projectors","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"","title":"Projectors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/clicks/#Click-States","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"","title":"Click States"},{"category":"method","location":"api/clicks/#QuantumInterface.projector-Tuple{Vector{Int64}}","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"projector(clicks::Vector{Int64}) -> ClickProjector\n\n\nThe detection outcome measuring clicks[i] photons in mode i. A Colon (:) marks a mode that is traced out instead of detected, so projector([1, :]) detects one photon in mode 1 and leaves mode 2 free.\n\nDetected outcomes are currently restricted to 0 or 1 photons per mode. Sum projectors with + to describe projection onto a superposition of click patterns, such as a parity measurement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumInterface.projector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/clicks/#Genqo.ClickProjector","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"struct ClickProjector <: AbstractClickOperator\n\nA Fock-space projector describing a detection outcome. A numeric entry represents a Fock state in that mode. An entry of -1 marks a mode that is traced out rather than detected.\n\nClickProjectors can be summed with + to describe projection onto a superposition of click patterns, such as a parity measurement. Only + is defined because scalar multiplication would not preserve the idempotent property of a projector.\n\nFields\n\nclicks::Matrix{Int64}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.ClickProjector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/clicks/#Genqo.freemodes-Tuple{ClickProjector}","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"freemodes(cp::ClickProjector) -> Vector{Int64}\n\n\nIndices of the modes left free (traced out) by a ClickProjector or a ProjectedPureGaussianState. These modes survive into the density matrix produced by to_fock.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.freemodes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/clicks/#Genqo.nfreemodes-Tuple{ClickProjector}","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"nfreemodes(cp::ClickProjector) -> Int64\n\n\nNumber of free (traced-out) modes, i.e. length(freemodes(x)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.nfreemodes"},{"category":"type","location":"api/clicks/#Genqo.AbstractClickOperator","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"abstract type AbstractClickOperator\n\nSupertype for operators diagonal in the photon-number basis. See ClickProjector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.AbstractClickOperator"},{"category":"method","location":"api/clicks/#Genqo.clicks-Tuple{Vector{Int64}}","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"clicks(cl::Vector{Int64}) -> ClickStateKet\n\n\nThe single-pattern click state with cl[i] photons in mode i and unit amplitude.\n\nSuperpositions are built by combining these, e.g. (clicks([1,0]) + clicks([0,1]))/sqrt(2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.clicks"},{"category":"type","location":"api/clicks/#Genqo.ClickStateKet","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"struct ClickStateKet <: AbstractClickState\n\nA superposition of photon-number patterns over a fixed number of modes. Each row of clicks is one pattern of non-negative photon numbers and coefs[i] is its amplitude.\n\nConstruct single patterns with clicks and combine them with +, -, and scalar * and /. adjoint gives the corresponding ClickStateBra. Click states are the targets of fidelity and the bra and ket arguments of dot.\n\nFields\n\ncoefs::Vector{ComplexF64}\nclicks::Matrix{Int64}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.ClickStateKet"},{"category":"type","location":"api/clicks/#Genqo.ClickStateBra","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"struct ClickStateBra <: AbstractClickState\n\nThe dual of a ClickStateKet, normally obtained as clicks([...])'. Supports the same arithmetic, and adjoint converts back to a ket.\n\nFields\n\ncoefs::Vector{ComplexF64}\nclicks::Matrix{Int64}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.ClickStateBra"},{"category":"method","location":"api/clicks/#LinearAlgebra.dot-Tuple{ClickStateBra, ClickStateKet}","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"dot(bra::ClickStateBra, ket::ClickStateKet) -> ComplexF64\n\n\nOverlap of two click states. Patterns are orthonormal, so this is the sum of amplitude products over the patterns the two states share.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.dot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/clicks/#LinearAlgebra.norm-Tuple{ClickStateKet}","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"norm(cs::ClickStateKet) -> ComplexF64\n\n\nEuclidean norm of a click state. Click states are not normalized on construction, so normalize a target before passing it to fidelity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.norm"},{"category":"type","location":"api/clicks/#Genqo.AbstractClickState","page":"Click States and Projectors","text":"abstract type AbstractClickState\n\nSupertype for photon-number (\"click\") states: superpositions of click patterns over a fixed number of modes. See ClickStateKet and ClickStateBra.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.AbstractClickState"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#api","page":"Overview","text":"The Genqo API models an entanglement source as a pure Gaussian circuit followed by photon-number-resolved detection. Gaussian states and unitaries come from Gabs.jl; Genqo supplies the detection layer and evaluates the resulting non-Gaussian state exactly, without truncating at low mean photon number.\n\nThe modeling process follows four general steps:\n\nBuild a pure Gaussian state with Gabs, optionally using Genqo's extra unitaries.\nDescribe the detector clicks with a projector.\nCombine the two with project. Nothing is computed yet. If loss is desired, pass a mode-wise loss vector as the η keyword.\nAsk for the quantity you want: tr for the heralding probability, fidelity against a target, dot for a single density-matrix element, or to_fock for a truncated photon-photon density matrix. Loading into quantum memories is also supported through duankimble and emissiveload, which return spin-spin density matrices.\n\nusing Genqo, Gabs\n\n# 1. A two-mode squeezed vacuum state\nst = eprstate(QuadBlockBasis(2), asinh(√1e-2), Float64(π))\n\n# 2-3. Herald on a coincidence click, with per-mode detector efficiency\nρ = project(st, projector([1, 1]); η = [0.9, 0.8])\n\n# 4. Probability that the herald fires\ntr(ρ)\n\nModes that are not detected are marked with a colon and stay in the output state:\n\n# Detect mode 1; mode 2 is left free and survives into the density matrix\nρ = project(st, projector([1, :]))\n\nfreemodes(ρ)          # [2]\nfidelity(clicks([1]), ρ)\nto_fock(ρ)            # a QuantumOpticsBase.Operator over the free modes\n\nA detection outcome that several click patterns satisfy is a sum of projectors:\n\nΠ = projector([1, 0]) + projector([0, 1])\ntr(project(st, Π))    # tr is additive over the summed patterns","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Performance","page":"Overview","text":"Evaluation compiles a symbolic moment polynomial per click pattern and contracts it by Wick's theorem. That compilation is memoized in a HybridProjectionEngine. By default, Genqo automatically manages a global dictionary of engines of various sizes. For finer control of the scratch space used by Genqo, an engine keyword may also be passed explicitly:\n\nengine = HybridProjectionEngine(2)\nμ = 0.01:0.01:0.5\nst = eprstate(QuadBlockBasis(2), asinh.(√.μ), Float64(π))\nPg = tr.(project.(st, projector([1,1])); engine)\n\nduankimble and emissiveload go further and return a matrix whose entries are contracted only when read (see Memory Loading).","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Contents","page":"Overview","text":"Pages = [\"clicks.md\", \"projection.md\", \"memory.md\", \"unitaries.md\", \"wick.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/sigsag/#sigsag_ref","page":"SIGSAG","text":"The sigsag module models a heralded dual-rail entanglement source architecture proposed by Yousef Chahine et al. as an alternative to the cascaded source architecture. The nomenclature of \"SIGSAG\" refers to the fact that this source could be implemented with a single Sagnac source.","title":"SIGSAG"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/sigsag/#Type","page":"SIGSAG","text":"","title":"Type"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/sigsag/#Source-Metrics","page":"SIGSAG","text":"","title":"Source Metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/sigsag/#Internal-Matrices","page":"SIGSAG","text":"","title":"Internal Matrices"},{"category":"type","location":"legacy/sigsag/#Genqo.sigsag.SIGSAG","page":"SIGSAG","text":"mutable struct SIGSAG\n\nParameters for the heralded entanglement source architecture proposed by Chahine et al.\n\nThe SIGSAG source is a heralded dual-rail entanglement source architecture proposed by Yousef Chahine et al. as an alternative to the cascaded source architecture. It can be realized with a single Sagnac configured entanglement source, hence the nomenclature of \"SIGSAG\" for short.\n\nFields\n\nmean_photon::Real: Mean photon number per mode (default 1e-2)\ndetection_efficiency::Real: Signal detector efficiency, ∈ [0, 1] (default 1.0)\nbsm_efficiency::Real: BSM detector efficiency, ∈ [0, 1] (default 1.0)\noutcoupling_efficiency::Real: Photon outcoupling / transmission efficiency, ∈ [0, 1] (default 1.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.sigsag.SIGSAG"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/sigsag/#Genqo.sigsag.fidelity","page":"SIGSAG","text":"fidelity(μ::Real, ηᵗ::Real, ηᵈ::Real) -> Real\n\n\nCalculate the Bell-state fidelity of the SIGSAG source under loss.\n\nComputes the Bell-state overlap ⟨Φ|ρ|Φ⟩, where ρ is the normalized photon-photon density matrix following heralding.\n\nParameters\n\nμ  : Mean photon number per mode\nηᵗ : Outcoupling / transmission efficiency\nηᵈ : Detection efficiency\n\nReturns\n\nReal-valued Bell-state fidelity of the SIGSAG source for the given parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.sigsag.fidelity"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/sigsag/#Genqo.sigsag.probability_success","page":"SIGSAG","text":"probability_success(μ::Real, ηᵗ::Real, ηᵈ::Real) -> Real\n\n\nCalculate the probability of photon-photon state generation for the SIGSAG source.\n\nParameters\n\nμ  : Mean photon number per mode\nηᵗ : Outcoupling / transmission efficiency\nηᵈ : Detection efficiency\n\nReturns\n\nReal-valued probability of successful photon-photon state generation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.sigsag.probability_success"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/sigsag/#Genqo.sigsag.covariance_matrix","page":"SIGSAG","text":"covariance_matrix(μ::Real) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\n\nConstruct the 24×24 covariance matrix for a SIGSAG source.\n\nParameters\n\nμ: Mean photon number per mode\n\nReturns\n\n24×24 Float64 covariance matrix in qqpp ordering after beamsplitter transforms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.sigsag.covariance_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/sigsag/#Genqo.sigsag.loss_bsm_matrix_fid","page":"SIGSAG","text":"loss_bsm_matrix_fid(\n    ηᵗ::Real,\n    ηᵈ::Real\n) -> Matrix{ComplexF64}\n\n\nConstruct the 24×24 loss matrix for SIGSAG fidelity calculations.\n\nEncodes per-mode loss: signal/detection modes (1, 2) use ηᵈ; measured modes (3–6) use ηᵗ. Added to the K-matrix before Wick evaluation of Bell-state overlap terms.\n\nParameters\n\nηᵗ: Outcoupling / transmission efficiency for measured modes, ∈ [0, 1]\nηᵈ: Signal detection efficiency, ∈ [0, 1]\n\nReturns\n\n24×24 ComplexF64 loss matrix for fidelity calculations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.sigsag.loss_bsm_matrix_fid"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/sigsag/#Genqo.sigsag.loss_bsm_matrix_pgen","page":"SIGSAG","text":"loss_bsm_matrix_pgen(\n    ηᵗ::Real,\n    ηᵈ::Real\n) -> Matrix{ComplexF64}\n\n\nConstruct the 24×24 loss matrix for SIGSAG probability-of-success calculations.\n\nSimilar to loss_bsm_matrix_fid, but measured modes (3–6) are traced out. Output modes (1, 2) use ηᵈ.\n\nParameters\n\nηᵗ: Outcoupling / transmission efficiency, ∈ [0, 1]\nηᵈ: Signal detection efficiency, ∈ [0, 1]\n\nReturns\n\n24×24 ComplexF64 loss matrix for probability-of-success calculations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.sigsag.loss_bsm_matrix_pgen"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/#legacy","page":"Overview","text":"The Genqo.tools, Genqo.tmsv, Genqo.spdc, Genqo.zalm and Genqo.sigsag submodules are the first generation of Genqo. Each wraps a single hard-coded source model: a parameter struct, a hand-built covariance matrix, and a fixed set of metrics computed from it.\n\nThey are retained because they are the numerical oracle the current framework is validated against, and because the Python wrapper is built on them. They are not where new work should start.\n\nwarning: Superseded\nNew models should be built with the v2 API, which composes arbitrary Gaussian circuits out of Gabs.jl states and unitaries instead of requiring a bespoke module per source. Everything the legacy modules compute — success probability, fidelity, spin density matrices — has a direct equivalent there.\n\nThe legacy modules build covariance matrices in qpqp ordering and reorder to qqpp with Genqo.tools.reorder before contraction, and they use the ħ=1 convention rather than the ħ=2 convention Gabs uses.","title":"Legacy API"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/#Contents","page":"Overview","text":"Pages = [\"zalm.md\", \"spdc.md\", \"tmsv.md\", \"sigsag.md\", \"tools.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/zalm/#zalm_ref","page":"ZALM","text":"The zalm module models a Zero-Added-Loss Multiplexing (ZALM) cascaded entanglement source. The ZALM architecture uses two SPDC sources, interfering half of the modes from each source on a pair of 50/50 beamsplitters to perform a Bell-state measurement (BSM). A heralding click pattern signifies a probabilistic photon-photon Bell state between the output modes. Dark counts and all three efficiency channels can be modeled.","title":"ZALM"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/zalm/#Type","page":"ZALM","text":"","title":"Type"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/zalm/#Source-Metrics","page":"ZALM","text":"","title":"Source Metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/zalm/#Internal-Matrices","page":"ZALM","text":"","title":"Internal Matrices"},{"category":"type","location":"legacy/zalm/#Genqo.zalm.ZALM","page":"ZALM","text":"mutable struct ZALM\n\nParameters for a Zero-Added-Loss Multiplexing (ZALM) cascaded entanglement source.\n\nThe ZALM architecture uses two SPDC sources, interfering half of the modes from each source on a pair of 50/50 beamsplitters to perform a Bell-state measurement (BSM). A heralding click pattern signifies a probabilistic photon-photon Bell state between the output modes. Dark counts and all three efficiency channels can be modeled.\n\nFields\n\nmean_photon::Real: Mean photon number per mode per SPDC source (default 1e-2)\ndetection_efficiency::Real: Signal detector efficiency, ∈ [0, 1] (default 1.0)\nbsm_efficiency::Real: BSM detector efficiency, ∈ [0, 1] (default 1.0)\noutcoupling_efficiency::Real: Photon outcoupling / transmission efficiency, ∈ [0, 1] (default 1.0)\ndark_counts::Real: Dark-count probability per BSM detector gate, ≥ 0 (default 0.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.zalm.ZALM"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/zalm/#Genqo.zalm.fidelity","page":"ZALM","text":"fidelity(μ::Real, ηᵗ::Real, ηᵈ::Real, ηᵇ::Real) -> Real\n\n\nCalculate the Bell-state fidelity of the single-mode ZALM source under loss.\n\nThis computes the overlap ⟨Φ|ρ|Φ⟩ of the post-heralding photon-photon state produced by the ZALM source with an ideal Bell state. The evaluation is performed via Gaussian moment (Wick) contractions using the ZALM covariance matrix, with efficiencies applied through transmission/outcoupling (ηᵗ), detection (ηᵈ), and Bell-state-measurement (ηᵇ) models.\n\nParameters\n\nμ  : Mean photon number\nηᵗ : Outcoupling / transmission efficiency\nηᵈ : Detection efficiency\nηᵇ : Bell-state measurement efficiency\n\nReturns\n\nReal-valued Bell-state fidelity of the ZALM source for the given parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.zalm.fidelity"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/zalm/#Genqo.zalm.probability_success","page":"ZALM","text":"probability_success(\n    μ::Real,\n    ηᵗ::Real,\n    ηᵈ::Real,\n    ηᵇ::Real,\n    dark_counts::Real\n) -> Real\n\n\nCalculate the probability of photon-photon state generation with the given parameters.\n\nParameters\n\nμ : Mean photon number\nηᵗ : Outcoupling efficiency\nηᵈ : Detection efficiency\nηᵇ : Bell state measurement efficiency\ndark_counts : Probability of click with no photon present\n\nReturns\n\nProbability of successful photon-photon state generation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.zalm.probability_success"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/zalm/#Genqo.zalm.spin_density_matrix","page":"ZALM","text":"spin_density_matrix(\n    μ::Real,\n    ηᵗ::Real,\n    ηᵈ::Real,\n    ηᵇ::Real,\n    nvec::Vector{Int64}\n) -> Matrix{ComplexF64}\n\n\nCalculate the density operator of the single-mode ZALM source on the spin-spin state.\n\nParameters\n\nμ : Mean photon number\nηᵗ : Outcoupling efficiency\nηᵈ : Detection efficiency\nηᵇ : Bell state measurement efficiency\nnvec : The vector of nᵢ's for the system, where nᵢ is the number of photons in mode i\n\nReturns\n\nNumerical complete spin density matrix\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.zalm.spin_density_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/zalm/#Genqo.zalm.covariance_matrix","page":"ZALM","text":"covariance_matrix(μ::Real) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\n\nConstruct the 32×32 pre-heralding covariance matrix for a ZALM source.\n\nParameters\n\nμ: Mean photon number per mode\n\nReturns\n\n32×32 Float64 covariance matrix in qqpp ordering after beamsplitter transforms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.zalm.covariance_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/zalm/#Genqo.zalm.loss_bsm_matrix_fid","page":"ZALM","text":"loss_bsm_matrix_fid(\n    ηᵗ::Real,\n    ηᵈ::Real,\n    ηᵇ::Real\n) -> Matrix{ComplexF64}\n\n\nConstruct the 32×32 loss matrix for ZALM fidelity calculations.\n\nEncodes per-mode loss: signal modes (1, 2, 7, 8) use η = ηᵗηᵈ; BSM modes (3–6) use ηᵇ. Added to the K-matrix before Wick evaluation of Bell-state overlap terms.\n\nParameters\n\nηᵗ: Outcoupling / transmission efficiency, ∈ [0, 1]\nηᵈ: Signal detection efficiency, ∈ [0, 1]\nηᵇ: BSM detector efficiency, ∈ [0, 1]\n\nReturns\n\n32×32 ComplexF64 loss matrix for fidelity calculations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.zalm.loss_bsm_matrix_fid"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/zalm/#Genqo.zalm.loss_bsm_matrix_pgen","page":"ZALM","text":"loss_bsm_matrix_pgen(\n    ηᵗ::Real,\n    ηᵈ::Real,\n    ηᵇ::Real\n) -> Matrix{ComplexF64}\n\n\nConstruct the 32×32 loss matrix for ZALM probability-of-success calculations.\n\nSimilar to loss_bsm_matrix_fid, but the signal modes (1, 2, 7, 8) are traced out. BSM modes (3–6) use ηᵇ.\n\nParameters\n\nηᵗ: Outcoupling / transmission efficiency, ∈ [0, 1]\nηᵈ: Signal detection efficiency, ∈ [0, 1]\nηᵇ: BSM detector efficiency, ∈ [0, 1]\n\nReturns\n\n32×32 ComplexF64 loss matrix for probability-of-success calculations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.zalm.loss_bsm_matrix_pgen"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/zalm/#Genqo.zalm.dmijZ","page":"ZALM","text":"dmijZ(\n    dmi::Int64,\n    dmj::Int64,\n    Ainv::Matrix{ComplexF64},\n    nvec::Vector{Int64},\n    ηᵗ::Real,\n    ηᵈ::Real,\n    ηᵇ::Real\n) -> ComplexF64\n\n\nCalculate a single element of the unnormalized spin-spin density matrix.\n\nParameters\n\ndmi : Row number for the corresponding density matrix element\ndmj : Column number for the corresponding density matrix element\nAinv : Numerical inverse of the A matrix\nnvec : The vector of nᵢ's for the system, where nᵢ is the number of photons in mode i\nηᵗ : Transmission efficiency\nηᵈ : Detection efficiency\nηᵇ : Bell state measurement efficiency\n\nReturns\n\nDensity matrix element for the ZALM source\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.zalm.dmijZ"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/tools/#legacy_tools","page":"Tools","text":"Covariance matrix utilities shared by the legacy source modules. The Wick contraction and projection machinery that used to be documented here now lives under the v2 API.","title":"Legacy Tools"},{"category":"section","location":"legacy/tools/#Covariance-Matrix-Utilities","page":"Tools","text":"","title":"Covariance Matrix Utilities"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/tools/#Genqo.tools.permutation_matrix","page":"Tools","text":"permutation_matrix(\n    permutations::Vector{Int64}\n) -> Matrix{Int64}\n\n\nConstruct a permutation matrix from a permutation vector.\n\nReturns the n×n matrix P where P[i, permutations[i]] = 1 and all other entries are zero. Used to reorder rows/columns of a covariance matrix between mode orderings.\n\nParameters\n\npermutations: Integer vector of length n encoding the permutation (1-indexed)\n\nReturns\n\nn×n Float64 permutation matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.tools.permutation_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/tools/#Genqo.tools.reorder","page":"Tools","text":"reorder(\n    covariance_matrix::Matrix{Float64}\n) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\n\nReorder a covariance matrix from qpqp to qqpp mode ordering.\n\nApplies the permutation [1, 3, 5, ..., 2, 4, 6, ...] via a similarity transform so that all q-quadratures come before all p-quadratures. Required before calling k_function_matrix.\n\nParameters\n\ncovariance_matrix: Real covariance matrix (or BlockDiagonal) in qpqp ordering\n\nReturns\n\nReordered covariance matrix in qqpp ordering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.tools.reorder"},{"category":"function","location":"legacy/tools/#Genqo.tools.k_function_matrix","page":"Tools","text":"k_function_matrix(\n    covariance::Matrix{Float64}\n) -> Matrix{ComplexF64}\n\n\nConstruct the complex-valued K-matrix used to form the Gaussian contraction matrix A.\n\nGiven a physical covariance matrix (in qqpp ordering), this function forms Γ = cov + (1/2)I, computes Γ⁻¹, and then builds the complex block matrix (and its conjugate block) used in the ZALM/SPDC moment formalism. Downstream code forms A = K + G, where G encodes loss / measurement modeling, and then uses A⁻¹ as the contraction kernel for Wick evaluation via W.\n\nImplementation note: This version is an unrolled/optimized construction that avoids intermediate block arrays and reuses an LU factorization for Γ⁻¹.\n\nParameters\n\ncovariance_matrix : Real covariance matrix in qqpp ordering\n\nReturns\n\nA ComplexF64 matrix K (block diagonal [BB, conj(BB)]) suitable for A = K + loss_matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.tools.k_function_matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"api/memory/#api_memory","page":"Memory Loading","text":"duankimble and emissiveload load a heralded photonic state into quantum memories and return the resulting spin density matrix as a QuantumOpticsBase.Operator.\n\nBoth return an operator backed by a LazyDensityMatrix: entries are contracted on first access and memoized thereafter. This behaves like an ordinary dense operator, but skips the entries a calculation never reads — tr(ρ) touches only the M diagonal entries out of M². Any whole-matrix operation materializes it, so arithmetic such as ρ * 4 or ρ ./= p yields an ordinary dense operator rather than another lazy wrapper.\n\nρ = duankimble(project(st, Π), [1, 1])\n\ntr(ρ)              # contracts only the diagonal\nncomputed(ρ.data)  # how many entries have actually been evaluated","title":"Memory Loading"},{"category":"section","location":"api/memory/#Loading-Models","page":"Memory Loading","text":"","title":"Loading Models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/memory/#Lazy-Density-Matrices","page":"Memory Loading","text":"","title":"Lazy Density Matrices"},{"category":"function","location":"api/memory/#Genqo.duankimble","page":"Memory Loading","text":"duankimble(\n    projected_state::ProjectedPureGaussianState,\n    d::Vector{Int64};\n    ...\n) -> Union{Operator{QuantumInterface.SpinBasis{1//2, Int64}, QuantumInterface.SpinBasis{1//2, Int64}, T} where T<:(LazyDensityMatrix{F} where F<:(Genqo.var\"#element#67\"{HybridProjectionEngine, Vector{Int64}, Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Int64, Vector{T}, Vector{Float64}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}} where T)), Operator{BL, BR, T} where {BL<:(QuantumInterface.CompositeBasis{Vector{Int64}}), BR<:(QuantumInterface.CompositeBasis{Vector{Int64}}), T<:(LazyDensityMatrix{F} where F<:(Genqo.var\"#element#67\"{HybridProjectionEngine, Vector{Int64}, Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Int64, Vector{T}, Vector{Float64}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}} where T))}}\nduankimble(\n    projected_state::ProjectedPureGaussianState,\n    d::Vector{Int64},\n    modes::Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}};\n    engine\n) -> Union{Operator{QuantumInterface.SpinBasis{1//2, Int64}, QuantumInterface.SpinBasis{1//2, Int64}, T} where T<:(LazyDensityMatrix{F} where F<:(Genqo.var\"#element#67\"{HybridProjectionEngine, Vector{Int64}, Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Int64, Vector{T}, Vector{Float64}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}} where T)), Operator{BL, BR, T} where {BL<:(QuantumInterface.CompositeBasis{Vector{Int64}}), BR<:(QuantumInterface.CompositeBasis{Vector{Int64}}), T<:(LazyDensityMatrix{F} where F<:(Genqo.var\"#element#67\"{HybridProjectionEngine, Vector{Int64}, Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Int64, Vector{T}, Vector{Float64}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}} where T))}}\n\n\nModels Duan-Kimble loading into a spin quantum memory.\n\nThe returned operator is backed by a LazyDensityMatrix, which builds and contracts only the entries that are read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.duankimble"},{"category":"function","location":"api/memory/#Genqo.emissiveload","page":"Memory Loading","text":"emissiveload(\n    projected_state::ProjectedPureGaussianState;\n    ...\n) -> Union{Operator{QuantumInterface.SpinBasis{1//2, Int64}, QuantumInterface.SpinBasis{1//2, Int64}, T} where T<:(LazyDensityMatrix{F} where F<:(Genqo.var\"#element#76\"{Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Int64, Vector{T}, Vector{Float64}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}} where T)), Operator{BL, BR, T} where {BL<:(QuantumInterface.CompositeBasis{Vector{Int64}}), BR<:(QuantumInterface.CompositeBasis{Vector{Int64}}), T<:(LazyDensityMatrix{F} where F<:(Genqo.var\"#element#76\"{Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Int64, Vector{T}, Vector{Float64}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}} where T))}}\nemissiveload(\n    projected_state::ProjectedPureGaussianState,\n    load::Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}};\n    ...\n) -> Union{Operator{QuantumInterface.SpinBasis{1//2, Int64}, QuantumInterface.SpinBasis{1//2, Int64}, T} where T<:(LazyDensityMatrix{F} where F<:(Genqo.var\"#element#76\"{Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Int64, Vector{T}, Vector{Float64}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}} where T)), Operator{BL, BR, T} where {BL<:(QuantumInterface.CompositeBasis{Vector{Int64}}), BR<:(QuantumInterface.CompositeBasis{Vector{Int64}}), T<:(LazyDensityMatrix{F} where F<:(Genqo.var\"#element#76\"{Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Int64, Vector{T}, Vector{Float64}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}} where T))}}\nemissiveload(\n    projected_state::ProjectedPureGaussianState,\n    load::Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}},\n    emit::Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}};\n    engine\n) -> Union{Operator{QuantumInterface.SpinBasis{1//2, Int64}, QuantumInterface.SpinBasis{1//2, Int64}, T} where T<:(LazyDensityMatrix{F} where F<:(Genqo.var\"#element#76\"{Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Int64, Vector{T}, Vector{Float64}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}} where T)), Operator{BL, BR, T} where {BL<:(QuantumInterface.CompositeBasis{Vector{Int64}}), BR<:(QuantumInterface.CompositeBasis{Vector{Int64}}), T<:(LazyDensityMatrix{F} where F<:(Genqo.var\"#element#76\"{Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, Int64, Vector{T}, Vector{Float64}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}, Vector{AbstractAlgebra.Generic.MPoly{Nemo.ComplexFieldElem}}} where T))}}\n\n\nModels emissive loading into a quantum memory\n\nThe returned operator is backed by a LazyDensityMatrix, which builds and contracts only the entries that are read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.emissiveload"},{"category":"type","location":"api/memory/#Genqo.LazyDensityMatrix","page":"Memory Loading","text":"struct LazyDensityMatrix{F} <: LazyArrays.LazyArray{ComplexF64, 2}\n\nAn M×M matrix whose entries are produced on first access by f(i, j) and memoized, so each entry is computed at most once no matter how often it is read.\n\nSee also ncomputed, duankimble, emissiveload.\n\nFields\n\nf::Any\ncache::Matrix{ComplexF64}\ncomputed::Matrix{Bool}\nlock::ReentrantLock\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.LazyDensityMatrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/memory/#Genqo.ncomputed","page":"Memory Loading","text":"ncomputed(A::LazyDensityMatrix) -> Int64\n\n\nNumber of entries of A that have been evaluated so far, out of length(A). Useful for confirming that a calculation really did skip the entries it never needed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.ncomputed"},{"category":"section","location":"api/unitaries/#api_unitaries","page":"Gaussian Unitaries","text":"Gaussian unitaries beyond those Gabs.jl provides, written in the same style and usable anywhere a Gabs GaussianUnitary is. Each is implemented for both QuadPairBasis (qpqp ordering) and QuadBlockBasis (qqpp ordering).","title":"Gaussian Unitaries"},{"category":"function","location":"api/unitaries/#Genqo.modeswap","page":"Gaussian Unitaries","text":"modeswap(\n    basis::Gabs.SymplecticBasis{N<:Int64};\n    ħ\n) -> Union{Gabs.GaussianUnitary{B, BitVector, BitMatrix} where B<:(Gabs.QuadBlockBasis{N} where N<:Int64), Gabs.GaussianUnitary{B, BitVector, BitMatrix} where B<:(Gabs.QuadPairBasis{N} where N<:Int64)}\n\n\nThe Gaussian unitary exchanging the modes of each consecutive pair.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.modeswap"},{"category":"function","location":"api/unitaries/#Genqo.greenmachine","page":"Gaussian Unitaries","text":"greenmachine(\n    basis::Gabs.SymplecticBasis{N<:Int64},\n    m::Int64;\n    ħ\n) -> Union{Gabs.GaussianUnitary{B, Vector{Float64}, Matrix{Float64}} where B<:(Gabs.QuadBlockBasis{N} where N<:Int64), Gabs.GaussianUnitary{B, Vector{Float64}, Matrix{Float64}} where B<:(Gabs.QuadPairBasis{N} where N<:Int64)}\n\n\nThe m-mode Green machine: the balanced beamsplitter network whose symplectic form is a Hadamard transform over the modes. m must be a power of two and must equal the number of modes in basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.greenmachine"},{"category":"section","location":"api/projection/#api_projection","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"project pairs a pure Gaussian state with a detection outcome and detector efficiencies. It computes nothing on its own — the result is a description of a measurement, and the work happens when a metric is requested from it.\n\nρ = project(st, projector([1, 1]); η = [0.9, 0.8])\n\ntr(ρ)                      # heralding probability\nfidelity(ψ, ρ)             # fidelity against a click-state target\ndot(ψ', ρ, φ)              # one unnormalized density-matrix element\nto_fock(ρ)                 # the whole heralded density matrix\n\ntr and dot are additive over the click patterns of a summed projector, so a herald that several patterns satisfy needs no special handling.","title":"Projection and Metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/projection/#Projecting","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"","title":"Projecting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/projection/#Metrics","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"","title":"Metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/projection/#Engines","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"Every metric accepts an engine keyword. Supplying one explicitly reuses its compiled moment-polynomial cache across calls; omitting it falls back to a process-wide engine for that mode count.","title":"Engines"},{"category":"function","location":"api/projection/#Genqo.project","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"project(\n    st::Gabs.GaussianState,\n    proj::ClickProjector;\n    η\n) -> ProjectedPureGaussianState\n\n\nApply the detection outcome proj to the pure Gaussian state st, with per-mode detector efficiency given by the keyword η, returning a ProjectedPureGaussianState.\n\nst must be pure with zero displacement, and st, proj and the efficiency vector must agree on the number of modes. Nothing is computed here; pass the result to tr, dot, fidelity or to_fock.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.project"},{"category":"type","location":"api/projection/#Genqo.ProjectedPureGaussianState","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"struct ProjectedPureGaussianState <: AbstractProjectedPureGaussianState\n\nA pure Gaussian state together with a detection outcome and per-mode detector efficiencies. Build one with project rather than calling this constructor.\n\nNothing is evaluated on construction: the object records what was measured, and the quantity of interest is then extracted with tr for the success probability, dot for density-matrix elements, fidelity, or to_fock.\n\nFields\n\nst::Gabs.GaussianState\nproj::ClickProjector\nη::Vector{Float64}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.ProjectedPureGaussianState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/projection/#Genqo.AbstractProjectedPureGaussianState","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"abstract type AbstractProjectedPureGaussianState\n\nSupertype for pure Gaussian states carrying a pending detection outcome. See ProjectedPureGaussianState.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.AbstractProjectedPureGaussianState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/projection/#Genqo.AbstractProjectedState","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"abstract type AbstractProjectedState\n\nSupertype for states carrying a pending detection outcome.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.AbstractProjectedState"},{"category":"method","location":"api/projection/#LinearAlgebra.tr-Tuple{ProjectedPureGaussianState}","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"tr(\n    projected_state::ProjectedPureGaussianState;\n    engine\n) -> Float64\n\n\nComputes the probability of success for a given projected pure Gaussian state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.tr"},{"category":"method","location":"api/projection/#LinearAlgebra.dot-Tuple{ClickStateBra, ProjectedPureGaussianState, ClickStateKet}","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"dot(\n    bra::ClickStateBra,\n    projected_state::ProjectedPureGaussianState,\n    ket::ClickStateKet;\n    engine\n) -> ComplexF64\n\n\nThe unnormalized density-matrix element of the projected state between bra and ket, which range over the free modes of the projection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.dot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/projection/#QuantumInterface.fidelity-Tuple{ClickStateKet, ProjectedPureGaussianState}","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"fidelity(\n    target::ClickStateKet,\n    projected_state::ProjectedPureGaussianState;\n    engine\n) -> ComplexF64\n\n\nComputes the fidelity of the heralded state against a click-state target, normalized by tr.\n\ntarget may be a ClickStateKet or ClickStateBra over the free modes of the projection, and should be normalized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumInterface.fidelity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/projection/#Genqo.to_fock","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"to_fock(\n    projected_state::ProjectedPureGaussianState;\n    engine,\n    cutoff\n) -> Union{Operator{QuantumInterface.FockBasis{Int64}, QuantumInterface.FockBasis{Int64}, Matrix{ComplexF64}}, Operator{BL, BR, Matrix{ComplexF64}} where {BL<:(QuantumInterface.CompositeBasis{Vector{Int64}}), BR<:(QuantumInterface.CompositeBasis{Vector{Int64}})}}\n\n\nComputes the unnormalized Fock-basis photon-photon density matrix of a projected pure Gaussian state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.to_fock"},{"category":"type","location":"api/projection/#Genqo.HybridProjectionEngine","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"mutable struct HybridProjectionEngine <: AbstractProjectionEngine\n\nReusable workspace for projections on an mds-mode circuit.\n\nThe engine holds the symbolic phase-space variables for the circuit and memoizes compiled moment polynomials (WTerms) keyed by click pattern, so repeated calls that share patterns skip recompilation. One engine may be shared across threads.\n\nEvery entry point (tr, dot, fidelity, to_fock, duankimble, emissiveload) takes an engine keyword and otherwise falls back to get_default_engine.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.HybridProjectionEngine"},{"category":"function","location":"api/projection/#Genqo.get_default_engine","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"get_default_engine(mds::Int64) -> HybridProjectionEngine\n\n\nThe process-wide HybridProjectionEngine for mds modes, constructed on first use and cached thereafter. Access is thread-safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.get_default_engine"},{"category":"function","location":"api/projection/#Genqo.get_phase_space_generators_half","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"get_phase_space_generators_half(\n    engine::HybridProjectionEngine\n) -> Base.Generator{UnitRange{Int64}, Genqo.var\"#get_phase_space_generators_half##0#get_phase_space_generators_half##1\"{HybridProjectionEngine}}\n\n\nThe symbolic alpha and beta-conjugate phase-space variables of engine.\n\nMost moments involve only these two, which lets the Wick contraction use the upper-left block of the inverse kernel rather than the full matrix. See get_phase_space_generators_full.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.get_phase_space_generators_half"},{"category":"function","location":"api/projection/#Genqo.get_phase_space_generators_full","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"get_phase_space_generators_full(\n    engine::HybridProjectionEngine\n) -> Base.Generator{UnitRange{Int64}, Genqo.var\"#get_phase_space_generators_full##0#get_phase_space_generators_full##1\"{HybridProjectionEngine}}\n\n\nAll four symbolic phase-space variable sets of engine, for moments that need the full inverse kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.get_phase_space_generators_full"},{"category":"type","location":"api/projection/#Genqo.AbstractProjectionEngine","page":"Projection and Metrics","text":"abstract type AbstractProjectionEngine\n\nSupertype for the reusable workspaces that evaluate projections. See HybridProjectionEngine.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Genqo.AbstractProjectionEngine"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#getting_started","page":"Getting Started","text":"","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Installation","page":"Getting Started","text":"Genqo.jl requires Julia 1.12 or later. Install it from the Julia REPL:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"Genqo\")\n\nGenqo builds on Gabs.jl for Gaussian states and unitaries, and returns density matrices as QuantumOpticsBase.jl operators, so one generally loads Gabs alongside it.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#How-a-model-is-built","page":"Getting Started","text":"An entanglement source in Genqo is a pure Gaussian circuit followed by photon-number-resolved detection. Building one follows four general steps:\n\nBuild a pure Gaussian state with Gabs.\nSpecify a detection outcome with a projector.\nCombine the two with project.\nAsk for the quantity you want.\n\nStep 3 computes nothing. The projected state is a description of a measurement, and the work happens only when a particular quantity is requested in step 4.","title":"How a model is built"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#A-first-heralded-state","page":"Getting Started","text":"Start with a two-mode squeezed vacuum state and herald on a coincidence click:\n\nusing Genqo, Gabs\n\nμ = 1e-2                                       # mean photon number per mode\nst = eprstate(QuadBlockBasis(2), asinh(√μ), 0.)\n\nρ = project(st, projector([1, 1]); η = [0.9, 0.8])\ntr(ρ)\n\nprojector([1, 1]) is the outcome \"one photon in each mode\", η is the per-mode detector efficiency, and tr is the probability that the herald fires.","title":"A first heralded state"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Leaving-modes-undetected","page":"Getting Started","text":"A colon marks a mode that is not measured. Those modes survive the projection and carry the heralded state:\n\nρ = project(st, projector([1, :]); η = [0.9, 1.0])\nfreemodes(ρ)\n\nto_fock gives the resulting density matrix over exactly those free modes:\n\nto_fock(ρ)","title":"Leaving modes undetected"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Building-a-circuit","page":"Getting Started","text":"As an example, we will show the ZALM source, which consists of two TMSV states, two mode swaps, and a pair of 50/50 beamsplitters forming a partial Bell-state measurement.\n\nfunction zalm2(μ, ηR, ηT)\n    st = eprstate(QuadBlockBasis(8), asinh(√μ), 0.)\n    apply!(st, [2,4, 5,7], modeswap(QuadBlockBasis(4)))\n    apply!(st, [3,5, 4,6], beamsplitter(QuadBlockBasis(4), 0.5))\n\n    η = [ηR,ηR,ηT,ηT,ηT,ηT,ηR,ηR]\n    project(st, projector([:,:,1,1,0,0,:,:]); η = η)\nend\n\nThe four detected modes in the middle are the BSM; the two outer pairs are left free and hold the heralded photon-photon state.","title":"Building a circuit"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Probability-of-generation","page":"Getting Started","text":"The probability that a particular click outcome occurs is simply the trace of the (unnormalized) photon-photon state:\n\nstate_heralded = zalm2(1e-2, 1.0, 0.9)\ntr(state_heralded)","title":"Probability of generation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Fidelity-with-respect-to-a-target-state","page":"Getting Started","text":"A target state is built with clicks and ordinary arithmetic. It specifies outcomes on the free (non-:) modes only:\n\nψ⁺ = (clicks([1,0,0,1]) + clicks([0,1,1,0])) / √2\nstate_heralded = zalm2(1e-2, 1.0, 0.9)\nfidelity(ψ⁺, state_heralded) |> real\n\nNote the distinction: projector describes a measurement outcome and can contain :, while clicks describes a target state and cannot. See Click States and Projectors.","title":"Fidelity with respect to a target state"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Sweeping-parameters","page":"Getting Started","text":"Everything broadcasts, so a sweep is a dot call:\n\nusing Plots\n\nμs  = logrange(1e-4, 10, 100)\nηTs = 10 .^ -([0, 3, 6, 9] / 10)     # transmission loss in dB\n\nF = real.(fidelity.(Ref(ψ⁺), zalm2.(μs, 1.0, ηTs')))\n\nplot(μs, F, xscale=:log10, ylim=(0,1), legend=:topleft, xlabel=\"Mean photon number per mode\", ylabel=\"Fidelity\", label = [\"0 dB\" \"3 dB\" \"6 dB\" \"9 dB\"])\n\nTransposing ηTs makes Julia broadcast it against μs as a second axis, giving one curve per loss value.","title":"Sweeping parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Loading-into-quantum-memories","page":"Getting Started","text":"duankimble and emissiveload load the heralded photons into memories and return the spin density matrix:\n\nρ = duankimble(zalm2(1e-2, 1.0, 0.9), [1,0,1,0])\n\ntr(ρ)\n\nThat matrix is evaluated lazily. tr needs only the diagonal, so only the diagonal was ever contracted:\n\nncomputed(ρ.data), length(ρ.data)\n\nIt still behaves like an ordinary dense operator, and any whole-matrix operation materializes it. See Memory Loading.","title":"Loading into quantum memories"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Where-to-go-next","page":"Getting Started","text":"The API section documents the full interface.\nFor the mathematical foundations, see [1].","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"#Genqo.jl","page":"Home","text":"Genqo.jl [1, 2] is a package for efficiently modeling hybrid Gaussian / non-Gaussian CV quantum optics. It is useful for problems such as modeling entangled photon sources, general GBS-based multimode state preparation, and photonic quantum computing.\n\nGenqo provides an interface for working with non-Gaussian projections of Gaussian states in a computationally efficient manner. In particular, the project function takes a Gabs.jl ::GaussianState and produces an intermediate of type ::ProjectedPureGaussianState, which performs no actual computations until functions such as tr, dot, duankimble etc. are called on it. Each method for this type implements a closed form involving a matrix Hafnian, which is handed off to TheEggman.jl for fast evaluation. This approach sidesteps any computations involving truncated infinite-dimensional density operators, which are slow, memory-intensive, and inexact. Presently, only projectors in the Fock basis are supported, corresponding to the outcomes of PNRs (photon number resolving detectors).\n\nGenqo also provides pre-assembled implementations of four common entangled photon source architectures:\n\nSource Description\nspdc Unheralded SPDC Source\nzalm Cascaded source [3] (single-frequency-mode ZALM [4])\nsigsag Alternative heralded Bell pair source [5]\ntmsv Two-Mode Squeezed Vacuum\n\nThese fixed architectures are the original interface and are documented under Legacy. The current API generalizes them: it models an arbitrary pure Gaussian circuit followed by photon-number-resolved detection, so a new source design no longer needs a module of its own.\n\nFor an in-depth presentation of the mathematical foundations of this package, see [1].","title":"Genqo.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Getting-Started","page":"Home","text":"See the Getting Started page for installation and a quick-start example.","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"#API-Reference","page":"Home","text":"The API section documents the current interface: build a Gaussian circuit, specify detection outcomes with a projector, and read off the metric you need.\n\nThe per-source modules in the table above are documented under Legacy. They remain the numerical oracle the current framework is validated against.","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"#Bibliography","page":"Home","text":"J. G. Richardson, P. Dhara, A. Bhatt, S. Guha and S. Krastanov. Full-stack Physics-level model of cascaded entanglement links. Advanced Quantum Technologies 9, e00809 (2026). Accessed on Apr 6, 2026, arXiv:2510.17976 [quant-ph].\n\n\n\nJ. R. Gunnell, S. Krastanov, R. M. Camacho and J. Gabriel Richardson. Computational Optimization of \"Genqo\": A Gaussian State Quantum Optics Modeling Engine. In: 2026 Intermountain Engineering, Technology and Computing (IETC) (May 2026); pp. 1–6. Accessed on Aug 13, 2026.\n\n\n\nP. Dhara, S. J. Johnson, C. N. Gagatsos, P. G. Kwiat and S. Guha. Heralded-Multiplexed High-Efficiency Cascaded Source of Dual-Rail Polarization-Entangled Photon Pairs using Spontaneous Parametric Down Conversion. Physical Review Applied 17, 034071 (2022). Accessed on Apr 6, 2026, arXiv:2107.14360 [quant-ph].\n\n\n\nK. C. Chen, P. Dhara, M. Heuck, Y. Lee, W. Dai, S. Guha and D. Englund. Zero-Added-Loss Entangled Photon Multiplexing for Ground- and Space-Based Quantum Networks. Physical Review Applied 19, 054029 (2023). Accessed on Apr 6, 2026, arXiv:2206.03670 [quant-ph].\n\n\n\nY. K. Chahine, J. G. Richardson, E. J. Katz, A. J. Fallon and J. D. Lekki. Heralded quasi-deterministic entanglement sources based on spontaneous parametric down-conversion (Mar 2026). Accessed on Apr 6, 2026, arXiv:2603.19489 [quant-ph].\n\n\n\n","title":"Bibliography"}]
}
